Here is an example of using strict equality to make a decision:

```javascript
const userRole = "admin";

if (userRole === "admin") {
  console.log("Access granted");
} else {
  console.log("Access denied");
}
```

In this example, the condition `userRole === "admin"` checks if the variable matches the string exactly. If you accidentally used a single equals sign (`userRole = "admin"`), you would be assigning a value instead of comparing it, which would not work for an `if` statement.

